00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.570
- And I worked with my brother
when we started VaynerMedia.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.220
And my brother and I worked
together on VaynerSports.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.910
Oliver, Sebastian, I
think the biggest thing

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.130
that people make the mistake
with family businesses

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.140
is a lot of things actually.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.840
First of all, if you're
capable of loving each other

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.290
more than the money and
the fame and the respect,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.490
that will always play out.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.570
I genuinely believe that
above everything else,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.790
like just truly valuing the relationships

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:40.950
with my brother and dad,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.930
like more than the money

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.200
and the accolades really, really mattered.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.920
Then the other thing is over communicating

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.920
to each other about dollars
and the kind of respect.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.030
One of the reasons that,

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.770
my brother and I worked so well together

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.780
was my brother was more
confident and more comfortable

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.280
with me being the front
man and getting the credit

00:02:07.456 --> 00:02:10.400
and he valued the dollars,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.260
not more, but like he was fine with that.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.290
And for me, I value the dollars
less than being the guy,

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:18.600
I wanna be the guy.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.850
With my dad, we had more of
that friction point of like,

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.250
who's the guy?

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.850
And that made it more difficult.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.450
But still we were kind of
able to overcome the friction.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.480
This is just ego and respect and money.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.270
Those are the two places
and that's why it's so hard.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.160
Most people actually care about money

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.110
and get into fights over it.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.460
Or they actually care about

00:02:47.641 --> 00:02:49.800
why did you sell all that merchandise?

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:50.720
Was it Sebastian?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.553
Was it Oliver?

00:02:51.553 --> 00:02:55.453
There's that ego and then
there's the legacy of it.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.082
It's balancing humility
and wanting credit.

00:03:00.082 --> 00:03:02.880
There's just a lot of
like real raw emotions,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.120
you know what I mean?

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.340
- Decision making is really tough

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.430
'cause we both have valid points.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.790
And I never would say that
Sebastian is wrong or anything

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.645
but I would say that

00:03:11.645 --> 00:03:14.370
when I come up with like
my point or my perspective

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.163
and he comes up with his perspective,

00:03:16.163 --> 00:03:18.693
it's really tough to
balance out each other.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.420
- You know what's really worked

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.420
for both of my family businesses?

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.660
Dividing and conquering.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.340
'Cause you can't have
two cooks in the kitchen.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.330
'Cause to your point Olive like,

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.470
it's not that you think Sebastian's wrong,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.570
it's that by you saying it should be red

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:33.930
and him saying it should be blue.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.024
Just in that small thing,

00:03:36.024 --> 00:03:39.010
when you're passionate about your thing,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.220
you wanna see your thing come to life.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.130
It's not even about being right or wrong,

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.120
it's not seeing it come to life

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.530
the way you envisioned it in your brain.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:48.363
- That's true.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.640
- I really mean this like,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.260
I think where people get caught

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.350
is they try to both do the same thing.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.633
And I think dividing
and conquering matters.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.010
When you have two cooks in the kitchen,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.817
you have no cooks in the
kitchen, just friction.

00:04:02.817 --> 00:04:05.170
For example, just cause
you guys are wearing it,

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.560
if you're doing merch, if I
was like the third brother,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.537
I'll be like, if I was
the third older brother,

00:04:10.537 --> 00:04:13.620
"Here's a strategy, we're
gonna make nine pieces of merch

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.190
and everybody makes
their own three pieces.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.010
And who gives a fuck if
the fonts are different?

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.753
And who gives a fuck if
like, you know what I mean?

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.540
That will work better because
then merit can breathe

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.660
versus it being convoluted.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.890
And what ends up happening
when it doesn't work

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:31.170
or when it does work?

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.607
When it doesn't work, you're doing this.

00:04:32.607 --> 00:04:34.620
You're like, see it was your fucking,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.917
even though both of your DNA is in it.

00:04:36.917 --> 00:04:39.170
When it does work, even
though it's blended,

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.060
then both are saying, this,

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.150
and you get into that friction point.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.120
And that's why I think
dividing and conquering

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.200
might really matters as well.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.253
Joint decisions are
impossible for a business.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:50.983
- Okay.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.910
- Back to sports, you don't
have two quarterbacks.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:57.910
You have two quarterbacks
but one on the bench.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.840
They're not both out
there grabbing the ball.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.370
Same with business.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.933
There is no co-CEO, it doesn't work.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:05.770
- True.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.650
- But what you could
do is if your partners,

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:08.933
is divide and conquer.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.793
You almost wanna write down every task

00:05:14.793 --> 00:05:17.040
and then pick one person to be one A

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.290
and one person to be one B.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.544
- Okay.

00:05:20.544 --> 00:05:22.730
- AJ and I had a really
good rule which was

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.550
if we actually, actually couldn't get

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.900
to a joint decision
through a conversation,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.930
I would make the final
call 'cause I was the CEO.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.742
That was basically the punchline.

00:05:34.742 --> 00:05:36.813
And that really worked.

00:05:39.246 --> 00:05:42.533
And he was great at like
kind of, sticking to that.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.530
With my dad, I really let
him work on the finances

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.270
and I kind of operated,

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.900
but it was his business and
he was used to operating.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.371
When he would kinda try
to make a joint decision

00:05:56.371 --> 00:05:59.510
or oppose decision or adjust my decision,

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:01.130
that's when a lot of friction happened,

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:04.283
especially with when you've
got employees underneath you.

00:06:05.678 --> 00:06:09.460
- How long have you been working
with your brother and dad?

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.070
- My whole life, in essence.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:12.940
Still to this day.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.032
Basically every
professional day of my life,

00:06:15.032 --> 00:06:17.280
I've been in that place.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.530
And I'll be honest with you,

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:22.403
when I'm most by myself, is
when it's most enjoyable.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:26.500
What I mean by that is,

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.240
in the years where my dad

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:32.680
was really letting me run
the business, it was great.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.470
Vayner was really easy, AJ
really gave me that spot.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.600
But even after he left and
went to run VaynerSports,

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.173
it was even better.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:45.503
It's hard when you're
one to share the ball.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.220
And it's not because you're
greedy or you're mean

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.220
or you're selfish.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.663
It's that, decisions drive a business.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:57.470
I have enormous conviction

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.420
and can make my decisions very quickly.

00:07:00.492 --> 00:07:04.123
I struggle with people that
don't have that clarity.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.560
I don't have clarity on other
people's decisions either.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.160
I think it's divide and conquer.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.527
What's been the biggest
friction point for you two?

00:07:18.527 --> 00:07:22.070
What's been the biggest fight
that you've had or about what?

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:24.310
- It's mainly when we just
have two different opinions

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.860
or something.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.806
We've definitely learned from
before a lot of different ways

00:07:29.806 --> 00:07:31.250
Work together.
- To work together and stuff.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.302
I mean obviously we still
go through our struggles.

00:07:33.302 --> 00:07:34.940
- Of course.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.983
- Most part, everything's
been a way better than before.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:42.070
- Do you guys have a good
sense of where you are?

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:43.330
If I said to both of you,

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.163
between money and like the credit,

00:07:47.475 --> 00:07:48.480
what do you guys say?

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:49.910
Oliver, Sebastian, if I asked you,

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:53.060
there's 100% for you,

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:56.520
money, credit, where
are you percentage-wise?

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:57.940
- Credit, money.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:02.280
- No, I would say I was more credit but,

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:03.600
- But real quick, real quick on that.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.140
It's not as simple as that.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.360
For example, when you say credit, money,

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.290
you might be 70/30 and he might be 30/70.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:13.880
And in that is the actual
unlock to your happiness.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.803
'Cause you're not 100/0
and he's not 0/100.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.560
But once you understand
that, it really helps.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:23.950
But I'm telling you by a country mile,

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:25.860
the number one piece of advice I have

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.720
is don't be in a position

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.790
where you have to make joint decisions.

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:31.840
Divide and conquer as much as you can.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.460
Where you have to make joint decisions,

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:35.883
see if you can do both.

00:08:39.468 --> 00:08:41.580
- That sounds cool, I like that.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.230
You see what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.453
- The more separated you are,
the more together you'll be,

00:08:48.038 --> 00:08:49.650
If you're like, "Hey, we wanna
make this piece of content."

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.100
Just make both of your
fucking pieces of content,

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.110
like literally.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:54.310
Even if they're similar.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.200
- I got you, I got you.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.670
- Good, good luck guys.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:00.579
Thank you.
- Thank you so much.

00:09:00.579 --> 00:09:01.746
See you later.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:08.553
- That's a good one.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:12.930
- Hey, what's up?

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:13.763
- What's good Richard?

00:09:13.763 --> 00:09:14.596
Good to see you again.

00:09:14.596 --> 00:09:15.630
- Yeah, I'm back again.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.477
Thank you for doing this,
super happy to be here.

00:09:17.477 --> 00:09:19.470
I'm gonna go straight to the point.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:23.900
I wanted to hear your take
on a quantity versus quality

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.380
of content for consistency purposes.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:29.670
My question is,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:34.670
is a lot of average content
better than great content?

00:09:34.849 --> 00:09:36.040
- Yes.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:37.690
- With a lot consistency?

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:39.050
Yes.
- Okay.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:42.050
- Here's why, who gets to decide
that it's average, Richard?

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:45.690
- Right, true.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:46.790
- Answer the question.

00:09:50.185 --> 00:09:52.670
It's only me who controls
like the content.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:54.940
- That's it, that's where
you have it mixed up.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:55.830
That's why I'm so excited.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.661
That's why I wanted to you to answer it.

00:09:57.661 --> 00:10:01.282
You don't get to decide,
the audience gets to decide.

00:10:01.282 --> 00:10:04.053
And you're gonna say no
for the audience too often.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:06.540
- Okay.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.800
- Creators, artists
say no for the audience

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.294
without the audience having a look.

00:10:12.294 --> 00:10:13.160
- That's true.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.410
- The end.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.350
That's why this is such
an easy question for me.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.470
(Richard laughs)

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:19.320
It's an easy question.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.000
It's just insecurity about the content.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.160
If you know people use perfection

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.400
as a disguise to not put out.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.640
It's actually being a cliche artist

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:28.417
and worrying about what people think

00:10:28.417 --> 00:10:31.390
and having some sort of
ideology in your own head

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.090
or just judging your own self.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.420
That's why I wanna push
everybody to put things out

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:38.080
'cause they will learn from
what the audience likes

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.300
instead of playing the game
of life in their own head.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:42.753
- Okay, true.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.840
As you advocate us to do
two, three poses daily.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.980
So I try to honor that flow,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.370
but I find it difficult sometimes

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:57.800
'cause I wanna do above average packaging.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.493
- But you are making
that up in your own mind.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:02.940
Like that's my concern.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:04.083
My concern is that,

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.720
I don't know if you even know yet,

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.090
if you haven't put out 100 things,

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.240
you don't even really know.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.143
Your below average might be above average.

00:11:15.001 --> 00:11:16.840
You just might be that talented.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.450
Or you may put out something
that's below average

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:21.750
but you read the comments
and you get an insight

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.250
which makes your next piece
of content way better.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:30.603
Most people do not respect the audience.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:33.541
It's a very interesting insight.

00:11:33.541 --> 00:11:36.300
I would argue that, what
really works for me,

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.110
is I overly respect the audience.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.400
All I'm doing is like looking
at the comments right now

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:43.740
while I'm on with you,

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.810
from Ontar Goodwin and Kareem Elrashidi

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.290
and Sam Reed and Mark Martinez,

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.223
I'm watching even when I'm creating.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.943
They're always right, we're never right.

00:11:58.261 --> 00:12:01.000
We might've been right
and then it plays out,

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.330
but we don't know if we're right
or wrong until it goes out.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.730
And no content ruins you.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.630
People are like, "Oh, if I put this out,

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:10.010
people are gonna think I'm
whack and then I have no shots."

